Lab: DOM XSS in document.write sink using location.search inside a select element
Vulnerability

DOM-Based Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)

Source
JavaScript
location.search
Sink
JavaScript
document.write()
Analysis

While reviewing the page source, I found that the application reads the storeId parameter from the URL and inserts it into the page using document.write().

JavaScript
document.write(...)

The value was placed inside a dropdown (<select> element).

Testing

I added a custom value to the URL:

Plain Text
?productId=1&storeId=satish123

The value appeared inside the stock checker dropdown, confirming that user input was reflected in the page.

Payload
HTML
"></select><img src=1 onerror=alert(1)>
Exploitation

The payload closes the existing <select> element and injects a new <img> tag.

HTML
</select>
<img src=1 onerror=alert(1)>

Since the image source is invalid, the onerror event is triggered and executes JavaScript.

JavaScript
alert(1)
Result

An alert box appeared, confirming successful DOM-based XSS.

Key Takeaway

When testing for DOM XSS:

Find user-controlled parameters.
Check whether the value is reflected.
Identify the source (location.search).
Identify the sink (document.write).
Understand the HTML context.
Escape the context and inject a payload.
Conclusion

User-controlled data from location.search was written directly to the page using document.write() without proper sanitization. By breaking out of the <select> element and injecting an image tag with an event handler, I was able to execute JavaScript and solve the lab.

